<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page1-utility-and-incomplete-info" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Utility, Lotteries, and Incomplete Information</title>

  <p>
    The games in this chapter have <em>incomplete information</em>: players won't know everything relevant
    when they make a decision. This is a lot like real-life situations that involve chance.
    The readings on this page mostly set up the tools we'll need. We'll start analyzing games on the next page!
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-4-1">
    <title>Section 4.1, Utility Functions and Lotteries</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 4.1.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      It presents two ideas:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            A <term>utility function</term> <m>u(s)</m>, where <m>s</m> is an
            amount of money received. It is increasing
            (<m>u'(s) \gt 0</m>) and concave down (<m>u''(s) \lt 0</m>), so
            each additional dollar is worth a little less than the one before.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A <term>lottery</term>: outcomes <m>x_1, \ldots, x_n</m> with
            probabilities <m>p_1, \ldots, p_n</m> summing to <m>1</m>. Its
            <term>expected value</term> is
            <me>E[x] = p_1 x_1 + \cdots + p_n x_n,</me>
            and its expected utility is
            <m>E[u(x)] = p_1 u(x_1) + \cdots + p_n u(x_n)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Take note of the formula for expected value! It's really just a weighted average (the same way your
    grades are computed in the gradebook). It will come up again when we compute payoffs later in the chapter.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-4-2">
    <title>Section 4.2, Buying Fire Insurance</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 4.2.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is technically a decision problem, not a game. To
      decide whether to insure, attach a probability and a payoff to each
      outcome, and compare
      the two options.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    We don't have anything to break down yet, because everything so far is getting us ready
    to analyze games in extensive form with <term>incomplete information</term>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Back in Chapter 1 we studied games in extensive form with <term>complete
    information</term>: whenever it was your turn, you knew every move made
    before yours, so you always knew where you were in the game tree. Now we
    allow chance into the game. Some of what has already happened may have been
    decided at random or hidden from you, so you may not know which node
    you're at.
  </p>

  <p>
    Poker is a popular example. After the deal you know your own hand but
    not anyone else's. What you <em>can</em> do is assign probabilities to the
    hands your opponents might be holding. Section 4.3 makes this more precise, so read it next!
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-4-3">
    <title>Section 4.3, Games in Extensive Form with Incomplete Information</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 4.3.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This will make more sense once we see an example on the next page.
      For now, just get the gist and keep track of the new terminology:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Nature</term>: nodes assigned to chance rather than to a
            player. The probabilities on Nature's moves sum to <m>1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Information set</term>: a group of a player's nodes that the
            player cannot tell apart. The available moves are identical at
            every node of the set, and a strategy must assign the same move to
            every node of an information set.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <term>probability of a path</term>: the product of the
            probabilities of Nature's moves along it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <term>expected payoff</term> of a strategy profile: sum the
            payoff of each complete path times that path's probability.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-4-1" iframe="question-form.html?section=4.1" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
